cl has no C++11 mode - its floor is /std:c++14 - and this case turns on a rule C++14 removed. [dcl.constexpr] in C++11 makes a constexpr non-static member function implicitly const, so cxx1 spells Board::twice ?twice@Board@@QEBAHH@Z with the B of a const member; cl, compiling as C++14, has no such rule and writes ?twice@Board@@QEAAHH@Z. Both are right for the language they are reading. clang under -std=c++11 agrees with cxx1, which is why tests/names.sh passes this case - and it is exactly the split CLAUDE.md describes: cl answers about the ABI and clang -std=c++11 answers about which language version a construct belongs to.
